{"symbol":"GRB2","resolved":{"input":"GRB2","symbol":"GRB2","ncbi_gene_id":"2885","name":"growth factor receptor bound protein 2","location":"17q25.1","aliases":["ASH","EGFRBP-GRB2","Grb3-3","MST084","MSTP084","NCKAP2"],"uniprot":"P62993","uniprot_reviewed":true},"function":[{"text":"GRB2 function (UniProt P62993, UniProt evidence code ECO:0000269): Non-enzymatic adapter protein that plays a pivotal role in precisely regulated signaling cascades from cell surface receptors to cellular responses, including signaling transduction and gene expression. Thus, participates in many biological processes including regulation of innate and adaptive immunity, autophagy, DNA repair or necroptosis. Controls signaling complexes at the T-cell antigen receptor to facilitate the activation, differentiation, and function of T-cells. Mechanistically, engagement of the TCR leads to phosphorylation of the adapter protein LAT, which serves as docking site for GRB2. In turn, GRB2 establishes a a connection with SOS1 that acts as a guanine nucleotide exchange ...","evidence":"experimentally validated","code":"EXP","citations":[{"source":"UniProt","id":"P62993","url":"https://www.uniprot.org/uniprotkb/P62993/entry","title":"GRB2: Growth factor receptor-bound protein 2"},{"source":"PubMed","id":"PMID:11016927","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11016927/","title":""},{"source":"PubMed","id":"PMID:11726515","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11726515/","title":""},{"source":"PubMed","id":"PMID:12171928","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12171928/","title":""},{"source":"PubMed","id":"PMID:1322798","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1322798/","title":""}]},{"text":"GRB2 function (UniProt P62993, UniProt evidence code ECO:0000269): Does not bind to phosphorylated epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) but inhibits EGF-induced transactivation of a RAS-responsive element. Acts as a dominant negative protein over GRB2 and by suppressing proliferative signals, may trigger active programmed cell death. Mechanistically, inhibits RAS-ERK signaling and downstream cell proliferation by competing with GRB2 for SOS1 binding and thus by regulating SOS1 membrane recruitment","evidence":"experimentally validated","code":"EXP","citations":[{"source":"UniProt","id":"P62993","url":"https://www.uniprot.org/uniprotkb/P62993/entry","title":"GRB2: Growth factor receptor-bound protein 2"},{"source":"PubMed","id":"PMID:36171279","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36171279/","title":""},{"source":"PubMed","id":"PMID:8178156","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8178156/","title":""}]}],"go":[{"text":"GRB2 GO biological process annotations: actin cytoskeleton organization, cellular response to ionizing radiation, epidermal growth factor receptor signaling pathway, insulin receptor signaling pathway, natural killer cell mediated cytotoxicity, negative regulation of natural killer cell mediated cytotoxicity, positive regulation of reactive oxygen species metabolic process, receptor internalization, signal transduction in response to DNA damage, T cell activation","evidence":"experimentally validated","code":"EXP","citations":[{"source":"UniProt","id":"P62993","url":"https://www.uniprot.org/uniprotkb/P62993/entry","title":"GRB2: Growth factor receptor-bound protein 2"}]},{"text":"GRB2 GO biological process annotations: positive regulation of Rac protein signal transduction, Ras protein signal transduction","evidence":"curated database evidence","code":"CUR","citations":[{"source":"UniProt","id":"P62993","url":"https://www.uniprot.org/uniprotkb/P62993/entry","title":"GRB2: Growth factor receptor-bound protein 2"}]},{"text":"GRB2 GO biological process annotations: regulation of MAPK cascade, signal transduction","evidence":"computationally predicted","code":"PRED","citations":[{"source":"UniProt","id":"P62993","url":"https://www.uniprot.org/uniprotkb/P62993/entry","title":"GRB2: Growth factor receptor-bound protein 2"}]}],"pathways":[{"source":"Reactome","gene"